# What never belongs in the build context.
#
# The build stage runs Gradle inside the image, so the context is the source tree and nothing
# that a previous build, a running node or an IDE left beside it. Keeping those out is not only
# about upload size: build/ holds jars compiled against another JDK, data/ holds a live engine's
# segments and write-ahead logs, and /excalibur.properties is the gitignored working copy that
# may carry a real EXCALIBUR_LLM_API_KEY.

# Build outputs. Anchored the way .gitignore anchors them: a bare "build" would also match a
# Java package named build, which this repository has.
/build/
*/build/
**/out/
**/target/
*.class
*.war

# Gradle state. The wrapper jar and its properties are required by the build stage and must
# survive the exclusion above them.
.gradle/
gradle/wrapper/dists/
!gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar
!gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.properties

# Version control and CI metadata.
.git/
.github/
.gitignore
.gitattributes

# Engine runtime state: segments, write-ahead logs, catalogs, per-segment index files.
data/
*.db
*.dat
*.wal
*.idx
*.csr
*.hnsw
*.lsh
*.hprof

# Local configuration. The working copy may hold a real API key. Anchored, so the packaged
# default at excalibur-api/src/main/resources/excalibur.properties - which the node falls back
# to from the classpath - is still copied in. The template beside it is not excluded:
# ConfigKeyInventoryTest reads excalibur.properties.example from the repository root, so a
# context without it cannot run this module's tests.
/excalibur.properties

# IDE and editor state.
.idea/
.vscode/
*.iml
*.ipr
*.iws
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# Logs, benchmark output and scratch.
*.log
**/jmh-results/
**/benchmark-results/
tmp/
temp/
*.tmp

# Documentation and the image's own inputs: none of it is read by the build.
*.md
LICENSE
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
